"""Original flooring planning and ordering cases, with established owner-budget methods."""
from pathlib import Path
import runpy,copy
base=runpy.run_path(str(Path(__file__).with_name('renovation-blueprint.py')))
CHAPTERS=[]
def chapter(title,deck,paragraphs,example,rows,conclusion,prompts,checks,task):
    CHAPTERS.append(dict(title=title,deck=deck,paragraphs=paragraphs,example=example,rows=rows,conclusion=conclusion,prompts=prompts,checks=checks,task=task))
def method(index,title):
    c=copy.deepcopy(base['CHAPTERS'][index]);c['title']=title
    replacements={'renovation':'flooring project','Make the kitchen usable for two cooks':'Make the living areas usable for the household','buy new cabinets':'order flooring','A bathroom project in a one-bath home has a different temporary-use problem from the same work in an empty property.':'Replacing flooring in every occupied room creates different temporary-use needs from working in an empty room.','fixture allowance':'finish-selection allowance'}
    def adapt(s):
        for a,b in replacements.items():s=s.replace(a,b)
        return s
    for k in ['deck','example','conclusion','task']:c[k]=adapt(c[k])
    for k in ['paragraphs','prompts','checks']:c[k]=[adapt(x) for x in c[k]]
    CHAPTERS.append(c)

method(0,'Define the flooring project brief')
chapter('Build a room and surface schedule','Give every quantity a location and a scope boundary.',[
'Draw a simple plan and assign a room ID to every space included in the project. Record closets, connecting halls, landings and other areas that might be missed in a room count. Identify the retained floors and the exact doorway or boundary where each new surface stops. Keep the dated plan with your photographs and proposals.',
'Record the existing floor, proposed product family and observed conditions separately. A visible stain or uneven area is an observation for the installer to assess; it does not identify the cause or the correct remedy. Keep reports from prior repairs and note which conditions are concealed until the existing covering is professionally removed.',
'Use the same room schedule for measurement, ordering, installation and completion. If a bedroom is removed from the project, revise each dependent record rather than subtracting an approximate area from one quote. Shared hallways and closets should appear once, even when more than one room uses them.',
'Decide which rooms must stay usable and what furniture or stored items occupy them. The plan should show temporary routes, staged work and any owner tasks agreed with the contractor. An area-only estimate does not tell you whether the household can function during installation.'
], 'A fictional schedule lists three bedrooms, one hall and two closets. The six space IDs include the closets separately; they are not already inside the bedroom measurements.', [('Bedrooms','3 IDs'),('Hall','1 ID'),('Closets','2 IDs'),('Total schedule','6 IDs')],
'A complete room count does not prove that the quantities or product choices are correct. It creates a shared index for checking them.',
['Dated plan / room IDs / project boundaries','Existing and proposed surfaces / retained areas','Observed conditions / reports / open assessments','Shared areas / closets / duplication check','Occupied routes / furniture / staging needs'],
['Every included area has a unique ID.','Observations are separated from technical findings.','The same boundaries are used for all bids.'], 'Send the dated room schedule to every bidder before requesting a final installed price.')

chapter('Measure net area without counting it twice','Keep dimensions separate from purchasing allowances.',[
'For a rectangular area, multiply length by width using matching units. Record the original dimensions and how they were obtained, then show the calculated area on the room schedule. Split an irregular room into non-overlapping rectangles rather than estimating one large box and quietly treating it as exact.',
'State how alcoves, closets, fixed cabinetry and other boundaries are handled. Do not deduct an obstruction automatically: the installer must confirm where the selected flooring will actually run. For a triangle, use one-half times base times perpendicular height, not the length of a sloping edge.',
'Keep net coverage area distinct from ordered coverage. The room measurement describes where the floor goes; the purchasing quantity also reflects the approved layout allowance, packaging and any separately selected spare stock. Adding those items to room dimensions can hide why the order increased.',
'Have the installer verify the field takeoff and the boundaries before material is ordered. A calculator checks multiplication, not whether a wall is square or a closet was included. Retain both the first measurement and the approved revision so later differences can be explained.'
], 'Two non-overlapping rectangles measure 12 × 10 ft and 4 × 3 ft. A separately measured 2 × 2 ft opening is excluded only because the installer confirms it will not receive flooring.', [('Main rectangle','120 sq ft'),('Alcove','12 sq ft'),('Confirmed exclusion','−4 sq ft'),('Net coverage area','128 sq ft')],
'The 128 sq ft is net coverage only. No waste allowance, packaging round-up or spare stock has been added.',
['Room ID / dimension source / measurement date','Non-overlapping shapes / lengths / units','Confirmed inclusions and exclusions','Net area calculation / independent check','Installer-approved takeoff / revision reference'],
['Dimensions use matching units.','Exclusions match the actual installation boundary.','Net area is separate from ordered coverage.'], 'Verify one room independently and reconcile every difference before approving the takeoff.')

chapter('Choose a layout allowance and round to packs','A percentage is an assumption to justify, not a universal rule.',[
'Ask the installer for a purchasing allowance based on the selected product, room geometry and actual layout. Straight runs, patterns, small rooms and difficult cuts can produce different requirements. Do not treat an example percentage from this book or a calculator default as an instruction for the project.',
'Multiply the approved net area by one plus the selected allowance fraction. Keep that result unrounded until dividing by the actual coverage printed for the selected pack. Round the pack count upward when whole packs are required, then multiply back to see the coverage being purchased.',
'Distinguish allowance from packaging surplus. The allowance is the planned addition for the layout; the pack round-up is a consequence of the selling unit. Neither automatically means that a full unopened box will remain after installation, because cuts and defects affect what can be used.',
'Have the installer and supplier confirm the order quantity, pack coverage and return terms together. If a product is sold by piece, roll or another unit, use that unit instead. Never compare a rate per square foot with a pack count without the conversion and the same scope basis.'
], 'For arithmetic only, an installer-approved net area is assumed to be 528 sq ft with an invented 8% allowance. Each fictional pack covers 23.5 sq ft and is sold whole.', [('528 × 1.08','570.24 sq ft'),('570.24 / 23.5','24.2655 packs'),('Whole packs required','25 packs'),('Purchased coverage: 25 × 23.5','587.5 sq ft'),('Coverage above allowance result','17.26 sq ft')],
'The 17.26 sq ft comes from pack rounding. The 8% allowance is a teaching input, not a recommendation for any flooring.',
['Approved net area / room schedule version','Layout allowance / rationale / approving installer','Exact product / pack coverage / selling unit','Unrounded calculation / rounded pack count','Purchased coverage / supplier and installer confirmation'],
['The allowance has a project-specific basis.','Whole-pack rounding is upward.','Packaging surplus is not promised usable spare stock.'], 'Ask the supplier to confirm coverage per pack on the written order before payment.')

chapter('Specify the product and compare equal units','A sample name alone is not an order specification.',[
'Record manufacturer, collection, product code, color, dimensions and other identifiers from the selected product documentation. Link the approved sample to that exact selection. A different thickness or construction carrying a similar color name can change accessories, installation scope and the complete price.',
'Ask the supplier and installer to confirm suitability for the proposed rooms and conditions. Keep their assessment with the current product instructions. Shaw publishes separate installation resources for different product types [2]; a general flooring label cannot establish one set of instructions for all products.',
'Normalize price units before comparing. A quotation may show dollars per square foot, dollars per square yard, dollars per box or an installed package. One square yard equals nine square feet. State whether the charge applies to net area or purchased area and whether it includes installation.',
'Identify any substitution process before placing the order. A backordered product should not be replaced solely because the substitute has the same price or appearance. Obtain an updated suitability review, pack quantity, accessory list and written owner approval for the actual alternative.'
], 'One fictional material quote is $3.20 per sq ft. Another is $28.80 per sq yd. Both exclude labor and use the same product and purchased coverage basis.', [('$28.80 / 9','$3.20 per sq ft'),('Material A rate','$3.20 per sq ft'),('Material B normalized rate','$3.20 per sq ft'),('Installed total','Not established')],
'Equal material rates do not establish equal delivery, return terms or installation scope. Compare those items separately.',
['Approved sample / manufacturer / exact product code','Room suitability / documentation / reviewer','Price unit / coverage basis / included work','Accessories and compatibility / unresolved questions','Substitution review / revised quantities / owner approval'],
['The order identifies the exact approved product.','Price units and coverage bases match.','Substitutions require a fresh review.'], 'Keep a copy of the approved product label and written selection with the order.')

chapter('Decide how much spare stock to buy','Repair stock and expected cutting waste are different records.',[
'Decide whether to retain additional material for possible later repairs and ask the installer what forms of leftover material will be useful. Short cut pieces and damaged boards may not provide the same repair options as unused full pieces. Do not count the entire allowance or pack surplus as guaranteed repair stock.',
'Ask the supplier about batch or lot identification, visible variation, later availability and the handling of mixed deliveries for the selected product. Keep these questions attached to the exact product and order. Buying extra can preserve options, but it does not guarantee a future repair will be invisible.',
'Price deliberately purchased spare stock separately from the installation order. Show the number of packs, unit cost and any additional delivery or storage requirement. If the owner chooses no extra stock, record that choice without treating an uncertain future product supply as assured.',
'At handover, count usable leftovers and retained unopened packs, save their labels and follow the manufacturer storage guidance. Confirm return eligibility and deadlines before opening extra packs. The actual retained quantity should replace the planning assumption in the final project record.'
], 'A fictional installation order uses 25 packs. The owner separately chooses two additional sealed packs at $75.20 each for possible future use.', [('Installation order','25 packs'),('Additional repair stock','2 packs'),('Total purchased','27 packs'),('Additional material cost','$150.40')],
'The two added packs are an explicit owner choice. They are not part of the example 8% layout allowance and have no promised future matching performance.',
['Installation order / allowance already included','Optional repair-stock quantity / decision owner','Pack cost / extra delivery / storage costs','Batch labels / return terms / unopened-pack handling','Actual usable leftovers / stored location / final count'],
['Repair stock is not counted twice.','Its purchase is an explicit choice.','Final retained stock is counted rather than assumed.'], 'Ask the installer to label the retained product and reconcile leftovers at completion.')

chapter('Define removal and disposal responsibilities','The old floor can change both price and timing.',[
'List existing coverings by room and state which layers the proposed removal includes. Clarify adhesive, underlay, fasteners, trim and disposal boundaries with the contractor. A removal rate without a description of the layers or disposal arrangements is not a complete scope.',
'Record unknown conditions as questions to assess before disturbance. EPA advises that suspected asbestos-containing material should not be disturbed and that trained professionals should assess relevant concerns [3]. Do not identify a material by appearance, sample it yourself or treat a flooring budget as clearance to disturb it.',
'Ask who arranges any needed assessment and how findings change the project. Keep ordinary removal costs separate from unresolved specialist work. A provisional allowance should state its basis, approval process and remaining uncertainty rather than implying that every concealed issue is covered.',
'Clarify occupied-area protection, debris routes, cleanup and responsibility for damage to retained surfaces. Record which tasks the homeowner has explicitly accepted. If a condition changes during work, use the agreed stop-and-review process instead of allowing undefined work to proceed under the original rate.'
], 'A fictional assessed ordinary-removal scope covers 300 sq ft at $1.10 per sq ft plus a fixed $90 disposal fee. The fee is expressly excluded from the unit rate; no hazardous-material work is included.', [('300 × $1.10','$330'),('Separate disposal fee','$90'),('Defined ordinary scope','$420'),('Specialist assessment or work','Unresolved / separately reviewed')],
'Do not add the disposal fee if it is already included in the rate. Unknown specialist work is not priced by this example.',
['Existing layers / room IDs / agreed removal depth','Pre-disturbance questions / assessment owner','Included removal / excluded specialist scope','Disposal / protection / cleanup / fixed fees','Unexpected-condition process / approvals / evidence'],
['Removal layers and exclusions are written.','Suspected hazardous material is referred for assessment.','Unit rates and disposal are not duplicated.'], 'Resolve material-assessment questions before confirming disturbance or removal dates.')

chapter('Resolve subfloor and moisture questions','Visible floor area cannot establish installation readiness.',[
'Ask the installer to identify the substrate, condition checks and product requirements that must be satisfied before installation. Request a written readiness record for the actual room and product. The owner record should name the person who assesses the conditions and the documents they use.',
'Separate known preparation from work that remains uncertain until coverings are removed. Include the affected location, measurement basis and decision trigger for any conditional repair. Avoid an unlimited preparation line that supplies neither an assumed quantity nor an approval boundary.',
'Request evidence and written acceptance for moisture, flatness and other product-specific checks where required. This handbook supplies no testing procedure, numeric threshold or repair specification. Requirements vary with the actual flooring system, and a single generic result cannot approve all products.',
'Record the review date, provider, findings, approved remedy and effect on the schedule. If a finding requires another professional, keep that referral open until resolved. Material should not be treated as ready to install simply because it has arrived or the owner has already paid for it.'
], 'An invented provisional preparation allowance is 100 sq ft at $2 per sq ft. A later assessed and approved scope is 140 sq ft at the same rate, with no other changes.', [('Original allowance','$200'),('Approved revised scope','$280'),('Increase to forecast','$80'),('Technical remedy','Defined by responsible provider')],
'The arithmetic shows an allowance revision, not how to prepare a floor. Replace the old allowance with the revised cost rather than adding both.',
['Substrate / room / selected product requirements','Readiness assessor / test and inspection records','Known preparation / conditional quantity / unit rate','Approved remedy / responsible provider / schedule','Old allowance / revised scope / forecast replacement'],
['Readiness has supporting evidence.','No generic moisture or flatness limit is assumed.','The revised cost replaces the original allowance.'], 'Obtain the product-specific readiness decision and approved preparation scope before installation.')

chapter('Coordinate underlay, adhesives and interfaces','Accessories are part of a compatible installed system.',[
'Ask the installer to provide the complete proposed assembly, including any underlay, adhesive, moisture-related components and interface accessories. Identify which items are integrated into the selected product and which are separate. An extra layer is not automatically compatible or useful.',
'Keep acoustic, heating, apartment approval and other project requirements with the assembly record. Where a building manager or other responsible party must approve the work, identify the required documents and decision dates. A product marketing label should not stand in for confirmation that the installed assembly meets the actual requirement.',
'Use the manufacturer documentation and responsible installer assessment for compatibility and installation decisions. This book does not specify adhesive types, underlay thickness, sound performance or heating settings. Record exact product references and the professional explanation of how the proposed components work together.',
'Reconcile accessories across supply and installation quotes. A package may already include the adhesive or underlay. Confirm both the quantity basis and the responsible supplier before adding a separate allowance. Recheck the assembly when the floor product is substituted.'
], 'A fictional compatible assembly proposal lists $180 underlay and $120 separate adhesive. The installed labor package excludes both supplies; each appears once.', [('Underlay supply','$180'),('Adhesive supply','$120'),('Accessory subtotal','$300'),('Double-counted supplies','$0 after reconciliation')],
'This example does not prescribe an assembly. The selected products and site requirements determine which components are appropriate.',
['Selected floor / complete assembly / document versions','Underlay / adhesive / integrated layers / product IDs','Heating / acoustic / approval questions and reviewers','Supply owner / labor scope / quantity basis','Compatibility confirmation / duplication check / changes'],
['Assembly suitability has a responsible reviewer.','Accessories are not priced twice.','Substitutions trigger a compatibility review.'], 'Ask for one complete assembly schedule that both the supplier and installer recognize.')

chapter('Count trims, transitions and stairs separately','A room area does not describe every finishing item.',[
'Mark doorways, floor changes, exposed edges and stairs on the room plan. Identify where existing trim remains, is removed and reset, or is replaced. Keep counted items separate from linear-foot measurements and square-foot flooring quantities.',
'Ask the installer to confirm heights, clearances, profiles and details for the actual interfaces. Stairs and changes in level need project-specific assessment and appropriate requirements. This handbook does not design a stair, prescribe an edge detail or authorize changes to doors or fixed equipment.',
'Record the exact selected profiles, finish references and availability. A matching product range may not include every needed accessory. Clarify responsibility for finishing, painting, disposal and any work by another trade before using a trim-only supply number as a complete price.',
'Check whether labor is included per piece, per linear foot or inside an installation package. Compare quantities and units against the same plan and keep special areas as explicit line items. Doorway counts can change when the installation boundary changes, even if the main room area barely changes.'
], 'A fictional accessory scope contains three transitions at $35 each and 80 linear ft of trim at $2 per linear ft. Both prices are supply only.', [('3 × $35 transitions','$105'),('80 × $2 trim','$160'),('Accessory supply subtotal','$265'),('Installation and finishing','Not included')],
'The $265 is not the installed finishing price. Confirm labor, profiles, interfaces and any other trade work before comparing a complete proposal.',
['Plan locations / transitions / edges / stair IDs','Counts / linear feet / units / measurement source','Profiles / finishes / installer suitability review','Removal / reset / install / paint responsibilities','Supply and labor pricing / package inclusions'],
['Pieces and linear feet remain separate units.','Stair and clearance decisions are professionally assessed.','Supply-only prices are not labeled installed totals.'], 'Walk through every threshold and edge with the installer using the marked plan.')

chapter('Check roll width, seams and patterned layouts','Purchased coverage can differ from net room area.',[
'Ask for an installer-prepared layout where a roll product, seam plan or repeating pattern affects the order. A square-foot room total cannot identify the required roll cuts, seam positions or pattern alignment. Keep the layout drawing and roll or product width with the quantity record.',
'Distinguish square feet from linear feet and linear yards of a specified roll width. Multiplying width by purchased length gives nominal coverage area, but usable layout coverage may be lower. Compare quotes using the same material unit and stated layout assumptions.',
'Record seam locations, pattern choices and appearance expectations before accepting the order. Review how direction, adjacent rooms and obstructions affect those choices with the installer. This handbook provides no seaming, cutting, bonding or pattern-matching installation procedure.',
'If the selection or layout changes, have the installer revise the takeoff and total installed price. Do not apply the plank allowance formula blindly to a roll or a pattern-sensitive design. Label offcuts and spare material separately rather than promising that all purchased coverage will become usable floor.'
], 'A purely geometric example assumes a 12 ft-wide roll and a purchased length of 18 ft. It does not establish a suitable layout or seam plan for any room.', [('12 × 18','216 sq ft nominal coverage'),('18 linear ft / 3','6 linear yd of this roll'),('216 / 9','24 sq yd area'),('Usable installed coverage','Requires approved layout')],
'Six linear yards of a 12 ft roll and 24 square yards of area describe the same nominal purchase differently. They are not interchangeable units.',
['Product width / pattern / room plan version','Installer layout / cuts / seam and direction references','Selling unit / length / nominal coverage conversion','Usable coverage / offcuts / spare-stock distinction','Layout changes / revised takeoff / approval'],
['Linear yards and square yards are distinguished.','The installer supplies the actual layout.','Nominal area is not promised usable coverage.'], 'Approve the layout and its exact selling-unit conversion before authorizing a roll order.')

chapter('Organize delivery, storage and occupied rooms','Plan the handoffs around the installation, not just the date.',[
'Confirm stock, delivery terms, receiving responsibility and the place where materials can be held under the selected manufacturer requirements. Ask the installer to verify any conditioning or acclimation requirements for the actual product. Do not invent one waiting period for every flooring type.',
'Agree who inspects delivered labels, quantities and visible condition before installation. Keep the receipt and a record of discrepancies. Ask the supplier how a shortage, damaged pack or wrong product should be reported and who must authorize the next step.',
'Write the household staging plan: furniture moves, contents, temporary access and return-to-use instructions. Identify appliances or fixtures needing a separate qualified provider. An owner should not assume that a flooring crew price includes moving or reconnecting every item in the room.',
'Tie dates to readiness gates rather than a calendar alone. Supply confirmation, room clearance, substrate approval and other dependencies should have named owners. If a gate fails, record the schedule decision and approved cost effect using the agreed terms.'
], 'A fictional occupancy plan adds $240 furniture moving, $60 delivery and $90 temporary storage. The contractor confirms these are excluded from all other lines.', [('Furniture moving','$240'),('Delivery','$60'),('Storage','$90'),('Separate logistics total','$390')],
'The total is an invented planning example. Check whether a package already includes any of these services before adding them.',
['Stock / delivery / receiving owner / claim process','Storage and conditioning requirements / verifier','Furniture / appliance / household staging owners','Readiness gates / inspection / return-to-use guidance','Separate logistics costs / dates / delay terms'],
['Requirements belong to the selected product.','Receiving and shortage decisions have an owner.','Occupied-room costs are included once.'], 'Confirm the receiving checklist and room-clearance responsibilities before booking the installation date.')

chapter('Compare the complete installed proposal','Material price is one line in the project decision.',[
'Build a bid comparison from the same room plan, selected product, approved takeoff and defined assembly. List removal, disposal, preparation, material, accessories, installation, logistics and completion scope as applicable. Mark each line included, excluded, allowance or unresolved.',
'Distinguish net installed area from purchased material coverage when reviewing rates. A contractor may charge installation on a stated area basis while the supplier sells whole packs. Both can be legitimate when explicitly defined; multiplying every cost by the pack-rounded coverage can create an unsupported labor increase.',
'Ask for the basis of any provisional work and the approval process when concealed conditions are found. Confirm tax, delivery, payment and warranty terms on the same comparison. An apparently cheaper quote remains incomplete if required work has no price or responsible provider.',
'Select the proposal using documented scope and suitability as well as total price. Save the accepted product, plan, rates, exclusions and change process together. The worksheet organizes the owner decision and does not certify technical performance or contract enforceability.'
], 'A fictional comparison uses $1,880 material, $420 ordinary removal, $300 preparation, $300 assembly supplies, $265 finishing supplies, $1,100 installation and $390 logistics. Providers confirm there is no overlap.', [('Material','$1,880'),('Removal + preparation','$720'),('Assembly + finishing supplies','$565'),('Installation','$1,100'),('Logistics','$390'),('Defined comparison total','$4,655')],
'This is a defined-scope comparison, not an all-in funding plan. Resolve excluded work, tax, owner costs and reserve separately.',
['Common plan / product / takeoff / assembly versions','Included / excluded / allowance / unresolved lines','Material coverage / labor area / price units','Tax / payments / warranties / conditional-work terms','Normalized total / remaining gaps / selection rationale'],
['All bids use the same boundaries.','Material and labor quantity bases are explicit.','The comparison total is separate from the funding ceiling.'], 'Return the completed comparison to bidders to resolve open scope before selecting a proposal.')

chapter('Plan care, warranty and final acceptance','Close the project with records the owner can use later.',[
'Request the care instructions and warranty documents for the actual product and installed assembly. Keep supplier and installer obligations separate, including contacts, exclusions and the process for reporting concerns. Do not treat a warranty duration alone as proof that every later problem is covered.',
'Walk the agreed scope with the contractor and create a dated punch list with room IDs and clear observations. Record incomplete work, missing trim, cleanup or documentation without diagnosing technical failures yourself. Ask the responsible provider to assess any performance or safety concern.',
'Reconcile delivered quantities, installed scope, retained spare stock, returned material and credits. Show whether a credit has been confirmed or only requested. Apply it once in the forecast and retain the supplier evidence with the final invoice.',
'Record the care handover, readiness for normal use, approved changes and final payment obligations. Save product labels, accepted plans and useful completion photographs with the records. A complete owner file makes later maintenance or repair conversations more precise, without promising that no defects will occur.'
], 'A fictional closeout has a $4,655 accepted scope, $80 approved added preparation and a confirmed $150.40 credit for two eligible spare packs returned. No other scope changes apply.', [('Accepted scope','$4,655.00'),('Approved addition','$80.00'),('Confirmed return credit','−$150.40'),('Reconciled defined scope','$4,584.60')],
'A requested return is not a confirmed credit. This example does not include tax, reserve or other owner spending.',
['Product care / supplier and installer warranties','Room-based punch list / assessor / resolution dates','Delivered / retained / returned quantities and labels','Approved changes / confirmed credits / invoices','Use guidance / final obligations / archived owner file'],
['Warranty roles and reporting contacts are retained.','Confirmed credits enter the forecast once.','Technical concerns have a responsible assessor.'], 'Complete the room-based handover and save a reconciled final record before archiving the project.')

